Someone else has mentioned privately that this criticism/justification
terminology should be explained and used more carefully, so I'll try.

It's true that pointing out lack of justification is a kind of 
criticism.  It's just not the only kind.  Other kinds include pointing
out contradiction with other beliefs, and pointing out uselessness.  
PCR says the latter kinds are more important than the former.

But to address Tim's question, I can think of two kinds of answers.
One is, if PCR people don't find lack of justification important,
and keep believing PCR, then that's how PCR survives criticism.
The other answer is, if something's true, but no one knows why, or
no one says why, then it's still true.

I think some of us could come up with reasons why we find PCR useful/
plausible (it's not exactly a matter of truth since it's prescriptive).
But those wouldn't be PCR's own reasons--it doesn't have any, and that's
probably good!

